"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][30]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M30-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M30-L1 – Bioinformatics and Omics Data<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Data Science in Science and Society<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20, M21, M27, M28.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Normalise omics measurements,\nmodel differences in expression and explain the limitations of\nbiological interpretation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Molecular measurements and study design<\/h2>\n<p>Omics approaches measure many molecular features simultaneously.\nGenomics data, for example, describe DNA variants; transcriptomics data\ndescribe RNA abundance; proteomics data describe proteins; and\nmetabolomics data describe small molecules. The observation unit is the\nbiological sample, not the individual gene. Repeated measurements of the\nsame sample do not replace independent biological replicates. After\nsequencing fragments have been assigned in RNA sequencing, the resulting\nmatrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas genes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand samples\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRaw counts depend on sequencing depth, sample quality and technical\nprocessing as well as expression. Before analysis, sample identifiers,\nreference genome, technical batches and groups must be comparable. Where\nbatch is completely confounded with group, the two effects cannot be\nseparated analytically.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Normalisation and differential expression<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001690_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Control has 40 and 80 reads; treated samples have raw counts 120 and 160, normalized to 60 and 80.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Control has 40 and 80 reads; treated\nsamples have raw counts 120 and 160, normalized to 60 and\n80.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: RNA read counts before and after normalization.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A simple teaching example uses two genes, A and B, and two samples.\nIn the control, 40 and 80 fragments are counted; in the treatment, the\ncounts are 120 and 160. Assume that independent reference measurements\nyield relative size factors of 1 and 2. The normalised counts are then\n(40, 80) and (60, 80). The log fold change of gene A is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>40<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.585<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(60/40)\\approx0.585<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile that of B is zero. The apparent tripling of A is therefore partly\nexplained by sequencing depth.<\/p>\n<p>A realistic model often uses a negative binomial distribution for the\ncounts, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[K_{ij}]=\\mu_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(K_{ij})=\\mu_{ij}+\\alpha_i\\mu_{ij}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA regression can set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log\\mu_{ij}=\\log s_j+\\beta_{i0}+\\beta_{i1}x_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the size factor and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the group indicator. With the natural logarithm,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_{i1}/\\log 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the log2 fold change. The offset is not treated as a freely estimated\ngroup coefficient. In practice, size factors and dispersion are\nestimated using many genes and/or replicates, rather than two isolated\nraw counts.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>K<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">40<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">120<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">80<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">160<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>norm<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>K<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>lfc<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.log2(norm[:,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>norm[:,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(lfc,[np.log2(<span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>),<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(norm,lfc)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From significance to biological evidence<\/h2>\n<p>When thousands of tests are performed, adjusted p-values, for example\nusing Benjamini–Hochberg (M6), are important alongside effect size and\nuncertainty. A small adjusted p-value does not establish the mechanistic\nfunction of a gene. Subsequent pathway testing must account for a\nmeaningful background set and dependencies. For predictive models,\nsamples from the same patient belong exclusively in one data partition,\nand feature selection takes place within training. In single-cell\nmeasurements, many cells from one donor do not constitute the same\nnumber of independent people. Genetic data can be highly sensitive;\nprovenance, access and consent form part of data management.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> A third gene has raw counts of 20 and 80, with\nthe same size factors. Calculate its normalised fold change and log2\nfold change.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> The normalised counts are 20 and 40,\ngiving a ratio of 2 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2 2=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNo significance test can be derived from this individual comparison.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/vignettes/DESeq2/inst/doc/DESeq2.html\">Bioconductor:\nAnalyzing RNA-seq data with DESeq2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · the section The DESeq2 model\nwas opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M30-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M30-L2 – Medical Data and Clinical Model Validation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Data Science in Science and Society<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20, M21, M27, M28.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Assess clinical model\nperformance under changes in prevalence and plan independent\nevaluation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Clinical question and data limitations<\/h2>\n<p>A predictive model is intended to estimate the occurrence of a\nspecified event within 30 days at a fictional hospital. The prediction\ntime must be fixed: laboratory results obtained after the event must not\nbe used as inputs. Electronic health records contain repeated\nadmissions, missing measurements, billing information and data resulting\nfrom treatment decisions. The absence of a test can itself be associated\nwith a patient’s clinical condition. Diagnosis (a current event) and\nprognosis (a future event) have different target definitions. Censored\nevent times require methods from M17. Randomly splitting individual rows\ncan distribute one person’s data across training and test sets;\npartitions by patient and separate temporal or institutional evaluations\nare preferable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Prevalence and predictive values<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001722_en.svg\"\nalt=\"At 2% prevalence the positive predictive value is approximately 14%; at 10% it is approximately 47.1%.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">At 2% prevalence the positive predictive\nvalue is approximately 14%; at 10% it is approximately\n47.1%.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Positive predictive value under changing\nprevalence.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In a wholly synthetic example, a test has sensitivity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Se=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand specificity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Sp=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor prevalence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe positive predictive value is\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PPV=\\frac{Se\\,p}{Se\\,p+(1-Sp)(1-p)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nWith 1,000 people and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthere are 80 true positives and 90 false positives;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>170<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.471<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PPV=80/170\\approx0.471<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe same conditional rates would yield 16 true positives and 98 false\npositives, giving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.140<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PPV\\approx0.140<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis calculation assumes constant sensitivity and specificity; both need\nto be remeasured if the patient mix changes. A high ROC-AUC alone says\nnothing about probability calibration or the number of unnecessary\ninterventions at a particular threshold.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.02<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> se<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> sp<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ppv<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>se<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(se<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>sp)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(ppv,[<span class=\"dv\">80<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">170<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">16<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">114<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(ppv)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>External evaluation and clinical decisions<\/h2>\n<p>A frozen model is evaluated at another institution without\nreadjusting it there. Eligibility criteria, time windows, data\nprocessing, discrimination, calibration, confidence intervals and\nperformance differences between relevant groups must all be documented.\nRecalibration constitutes a model change and requires another\nindependent evaluation. Decision analyses distinguish the benefits of\ntrue-positive decisions from the harm and effort of false-positive\ndecisions. The TRIPOD+AI reporting guideline supports transparency; it\nreplaces neither clinical effectiveness studies nor regulatory approval.\nThis numerical example is not a medical test or treatment\nrecommendation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Calculate the expected numbers of\ntrue-positive and false-positive results for 2,000 observations at 5%\nprevalence.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> Of 100 events, 80 are detected; among the\n1,900 non-events, 190 are false positives. PPV\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>270<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>29.6<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=80/270\\approx29.6\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38626948/\">Collins et\nal. (2024): TRIPOD+AI statement<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · PubMed abstract and\nbibliographical details<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M30-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M30-L3 – Physical Modelling and Inverse Problems<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Data Science in Science and Society<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20, M21, M27, M28.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Solve an ill-conditioned inverse\nproblem and understand regularisation as a controlled bias–variance\ntrade-off.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Forward model and inverse problem<\/h2>\n<p>In physics, the process mapping an unknown field to measurements is\noften known. For example, a sensor averages temperature along a path,\nlosing fine structure. In discrete form this is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=Ax+\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the quantity sought,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the measurement operator and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measurement noise. The inverse problem seeks to determine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSmall singular values of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstrongly amplify noise in particular directions. Rank deficiency also\nleaves some components non-identifiable. Even a perfect numerical\nsolution to a physically incorrect forward model can give an incorrect\nresult.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Tikhonov regularisation in a two-component model<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001599_en.svg\"\nalt=\"An estimated second component shifts from nearly 3 without regularization to 1.5 at lambda 0.0001; the illustrative truth is 2.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">An estimated second component shifts from\nnearly 3 without regularization to 1.5 at lambda 0.0001; the\nillustrative truth is 2.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Regularization of an inverse reconstruction.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For dimensionless teaching data, take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\operatorname{diag}(1,0.01)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the true solution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(1,2)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe measurement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.03<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=(1,0.03)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains an error of 0.01 in the second component. Direct inversion\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,3)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe minimise\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|Ax-y\\|_2^2+\\lambda\\|x\\|_2^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nThe normal equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(A^\\top A+\\lambda I)x_\\lambda=A^\\top y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives, for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0001<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.0001<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.99990<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.99990,1.5)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn singular-value coordinates, the inverse amplification\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis replaced by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma/(\\sigma^2+\\lambda)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStronger regularisation improves stability but can suppress genuine\nstructure. The parameter is not universal: measurement units, noise\ncovariance and variable scaling alter its meaning.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>A<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.diag([<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.01<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.03<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> lam<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-4<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>raw<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.solve(A,y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.solve(A.T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>A<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>lam<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.eye(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),A.T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(raw,[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(x,[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.0001<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(raw,x)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical constraints and validation<\/h2>\n<p>Instead of the identity operator, a difference operator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\|Lx\\|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan favour smoothness. Non-negativity, conservation laws or known\nboundary conditions can restrict the admissible solution space.\nPhysics-informed neural networks combine measurement errors with\ndifferential-equation residuals; the weights of different residuals must\nbe compatible with scales and boundary conditions. Uncertainty in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be distinguished from measurement noise. Independent sensors or\nsynthetic data with a known field help validate a reconstruction, but\nsimulations using precisely the same model can conceal model error. In a\nreal inverse problem, parameter selection based on the unknown true\nsolution would not be permissible.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> What is the second component when λ is doubled\nto 0.0002?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.01<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.03<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.0001<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.0002<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.01\\cdot0.03/(0.0001+0.0002)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe estimate is damped more strongly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/S0895479897326432\">Tikhonov\nRegularization and Total Least Squares (SIAM)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M30-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M30-L4 – Climate, Environment, Geospatial Data and Sustainability<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Data Science in Science and Society<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20, M21, M27, M28.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Aggregate gridded data with area\nweighting and avoid spatial and temporal validation errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Climate data as spatiotemporal measurement fields<\/h2>\n<p>A climate dataset describes, for example, temperature, precipitation\nor wind on a space–time grid. Weather stations, satellites and\nreanalyses are distinct data products. A reanalysis such as ERA5\ncombines observational information with a physical model, so its grid\nvalues are not directly measured temperatures everywhere. Before\ncombining data, check coordinate systems, grid resolution, measurement\nheight, calendars, temporal aggregation and units. Unlike instantaneous\ntemperature, accumulated precipitation must not be averaged without\naccounting for the accumulation interval. Temperature differences\nmeasured in kelvin are numerically equal to differences measured in\ndegrees Celsius.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Area weighting and anomalies<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001605_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Temperature anomalies 1 K and 3 K yield an unweighted mean of 2 K and an area-weighted mean of 1.67 K.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Temperature anomalies 1 K and 3 K yield\nan unweighted mean of 2 K and an area-weighted mean of 1.67\nK.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Area-weighted temperature anomalies.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Equally sized angular cells on a regular latitude–longitude grid\ncover different areas on a sphere. For equally wide, symmetrical\nlatitude bands with equal longitude steps, the weighting is proportional\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos\\varphi_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMore generally, cell area is proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda(\\sin\\varphi_{\\mathrm{N}}-\\sin\\varphi_{\\mathrm{S}})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConsider two equal-angle sample cells at latitudes 0° and 60° with\nanomalies of 1 and 3 K. Their weights are 1 and 0.5. Thus,\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mover><mn>6<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar a=\\frac{1\\cdot1+0.5\\cdot3}{1+0.5}=1{,}\\overline{6}\\,\\mathrm{K}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nThe unweighted mean of 2 K overestimates the area-weighted anomaly here.\nAn anomaly is the departure from a defined reference (for example, the\nsame calendar month during a reference period), not necessarily an\nabsolute temperature. Where cells are masked, compute the denominator\nover valid areas and report coverage.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>lat<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">60<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> w<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.cos(np.deg2rad(lat))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>weighted<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.average(a,weights<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>w)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(weighted,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(a.mean(),weighted)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Spatial validation and sustainability<\/h2>\n<p>Adjacent cells and consecutive days are often dependent. Randomly\nsplitting individual grid cells can make models appear successful even\nwhen they merely interpolate nearby observations. Spatial block\nevaluation, temporal holdouts and tests on extreme events provide\ndifferent, often stricter evidence. A flood model additionally requires\nhydrological processes, catchment areas and independent events; a\nclimate correlation does not establish a local cause. For sustainability\napplications, specify system boundaries, the comparison period and\nuncertainty in emission factors. An area-weighted temperature must not\nbe confused with population-weighted exposure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> A third equal-angle cell at latitude −60° has\nan anomaly of 2 K. Determine the new weighted anomaly.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+1.5+0.5\\cdot2)/(1+0.5+0.5)=1.75\\,\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://climate.copernicus.eu/climate-reanalysis\">Copernicus\nClimate Change Service: Climate reanalysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · ERA5 section\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M30-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M30-L5 – Computational Neuroscience and Behavioural Data<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Data Science in Science and Society<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20, M21, M27, M28.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Convert spike data into firing\nrates and assess what statistical neural coding can establish.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Neural and behavioural signals<\/h2>\n<p>Computational neuroscience studies mathematical models of nervous\nsystems and relates them to measurement data. Electrophysiology, for\nexample, provides event times for individual neurons or groups of\nneurons; EEG measures electrical potential differences at the scalp,\nwhile fMRI captures indirect haemodynamic signals. These methods have\ndifferent temporal and spatial resolutions. A study might examine\nwhether neural activity depends on a stimulus or a decision. Trial,\nsession, participant and neural unit represent different sampling\nlevels. Stimulus timing, movement and measurement artefacts affect\ninterpretation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Spike counts and firing rate<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001655_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Neuron A produces 16 spikes in two seconds (8 Hz); neuron B produces 6 spikes in two seconds (3 Hz).\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Neuron A produces 16 spikes in two\nseconds (8 Hz); neuron B produces 6 spikes in two seconds (3\nHz).<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Neuronal firing rates from spike counts.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A spike train is represented by a sequence of event times\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAcross\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepetitions of an identical time window of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe mean rate can be estimated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>R<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat r=\\sum_{j=1}^R N_j/(R\\Delta t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFour synthetic trials, each lasting 0.5 s, yield spike counts of 4, 5,\n3, 4 for stimulus A and 1, 2, 2, 1 for stimulus B. The rates are 8 Hz\nand 3 Hz. Under independent homogeneous Poisson processes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∼<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_j\\sim\\mathrm{Poisson}(r\\Delta t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe model-based standard error of the rate is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\hat r/(R\\Delta t)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor 2 Hz for A in this example. The model ignores refractory periods,\nvariable excitability and dependence between trials. A time-varying rate\ncan be estimated over shorter windows or through smoothing, trading\ntemporal resolution against higher estimation uncertainty.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>counts<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>counts.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>se<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.sqrt(r<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(r,[<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(se[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(r,se)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Encoding, decoding and behaviour<\/h2>\n<p>An encoding model maps stimuli and context to neural responses; a\ndecoding model attempts the reverse, predicting a stimulus or behaviour\nfrom activity. Good decodability is not proof that the brain uses that\nparticular algorithm. Time windows from the same trial must stay within\nthe same data partition, and testing generalisation to new participants\nrequires participant-grouped splits. A Poisson GLM with a logarithmic\nlink function can model rates as functions of stimuli and past activity.\nIts performance can be assessed using held-out data and log-likelihood,\namong other criteria. Consent and access controls are essential for\npersonal data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Across eight trials of 0.25 s each, a total of\n12 spikes is observed. What are the firing rate and Poisson standard\nerror?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> Exposure is 2 s and rate is 6 Hz; the\nstandard error is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.73<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{6/2}=\\sqrt3\\approx1.73\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch7.S2.html\">Gerstner et\nal. (2014): Neuronal Dynamics, 7.2 Mean Firing Rate<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · textbook\nsection opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M30-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M30-L6 – Digital Humanities and Text Corpora<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Data Science in Science and Society<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20, M21, M27, M28.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Prepare historical corpora\ncritically and interpret normalised word frequencies and OCR errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From source to corpus<\/h2>\n<p>Digital humanities use computational methods to study questions in\nthe humanities and cultural research. A digitised newspaper archive is\nnot a representative sample of a society in the past. What survives,\nselection for digitisation, place of publication and genre all shape a\ncorpus. Optical character recognition (OCR) converts scans into\nmachine-readable text; handwritten material may require handwriting text\nrecognition (HTR). Each document should have a stable source ID, date,\nprovenance, page reference, transcription status and, where possible, a\nlink to the original. Preserve the unmodified raw transcription;\ncorrections and tokenisation form versioned processing steps. Historical\nspellings should not be silently modernised.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Normalised frequencies and measurement errors<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001612_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A raw count increases from 100 to 150 while the normalized frequency falls from 5 to 3 per thousand tokens.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A raw count increases from 100 to 150\nwhile the normalized frequency falls from 5 to 3 per thousand\ntokens.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Absolute versus relative word frequencies.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Two fictional subcorpora contain 20,000 and 50,000 words\nrespectively. The word ‘Garten’ occurs 100 and 150 times. Absolute\ncounts rise by 50%, but the relative frequencies per thousand tokens are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>20000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000\\cdot100/20000=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>50000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000\\cdot150/50000=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nClaims about language use therefore depend on the denominator and corpus\ncomposition. In a manually checked reference sample of 2,000 characters\nwith 40 substitutions, 10 deletions and 6 insertions, the character\nerror rate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.8<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">CER=(40+10+6)/2000=2.8\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe errors are derived from a minimum edit alignment. Words in rare\nscripts or damaged pages may be disproportionately affected. CER alone\nis not a measure of the reliability of any particular word count.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>freq<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">150<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">20000<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">50000<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>cer<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">40<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2000<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(freq,[<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(cer,<span class=\"fl\">.028<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(freq,cer)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distant reading, close reading and source criticism<\/h2>\n<p>Collocations, topic models, embeddings and named-entity recognition\nhelp researchers select potentially interesting passages.\nInterpretations must be traced back to specific passages. A change in\nmeaning over time may accompany a constant frequency; shifts in genre\nmay create trends without any change in society. Useful safeguards\ninclude stratified subcorpora, manual samples for each period and\nsensitivity analyses for tokenisation or OCR corrections. The frequency\nof a term is not automatically the frequency of the event it describes.\nCopyright, archive conditions and sensitive personal information must be\nchecked before sharing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> A third corpus contains 90 hits in 30,000\ntokens. What is its frequency, and which earlier corpus does it\nmatch?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>30000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000\\cdot90/30000=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper thousand tokens, matching the second corpus without establishing\nidentical meanings.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://programminghistorian.org/en/lessons/generating-an-ordered-data-set-from-an-OCR-text-file\">Crump\n(2014): Generating an Ordered Data Set from an OCR Text File<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nintroduction and learning objectives opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M30-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M30-L7 – Social Research, Survey Data and Weighting<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Data Science in Science and Society<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20, M21, M27, M28.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply design weights and\ncalibration and distinguish sampling error from systematic\nnon-response.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Target population and selection mechanism<\/h2>\n<p>Social surveys aim to describe attitudes, living conditions or\nbehaviours in a defined population. A sampling frame may exclude people;\nself-selection, non-response and ambiguous questions can also cause\nbias. A dataset with many responses is not necessarily representative.\nIn a probability sample, unit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a known positive inclusion probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts design weight\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_i=1/\\pi_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorrects unequal selection probabilities. The Horvitz–Thompson estimator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat Y=\\sum_{i\\in s}d_i y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis design-unbiased for the population total if all units can be reached\nand their values observed. For a mean with known population size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat Y/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be used. A ratio with an estimated denominator, however, is\ngenerally not exactly unbiased.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Weighted estimation in two strata<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001522_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Positive response shares of 70% and 30% combine to 50% without weights and 54% with design weights.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Positive response shares of 70% and 30%\ncombine to 50% without weights and 54% with design weights.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Survey strata and design weights.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A synthetic population has 600 people in region A and 400 in region\nB. From each region, 100 people are selected by simple random sampling\nwithout replacement, and all respond. In A, 70 answer yes to a question,\ncompared with 30 in B. The inclusion probabilities are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the weights are 6 and 4. The unweighted proportion is 0.5, but the\nweighted estimate is\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>70<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.54<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{6\\cdot70+4\\cdot30}{6\\cdot100+4\\cdot100}=0.54.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nThe difference results from disproportionate stratification. For stratum\nproportions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat p_h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na design-based variance can be approximated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msubsup><mi>s<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_h (N_h/N)^2(1-n_h/N_h)s_h^2/n_h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_h^2=n_h\\hat p_h(1-\\hat p_h)/(n_h-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the sample variance of a binary response. Cluster samples require a\ndifferent variance calculation.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>N<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">600<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">400<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> yes<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">70<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>N<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>yes<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>estimate<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(w<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>yes)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s2<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(n<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>se<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.sqrt(np.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>((N<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>())<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s2<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(estimate,<span class=\"fl\">.54<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.02<\/span><span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>se<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.04<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(estimate,se)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Non-response, calibration and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Non-response may be adjusted through inverse estimated response\nprobabilities if the assumed model is adequate and response\nprobabilities are positive. Calibration alters weights so that weighted\ntotals for known auxiliary characteristics agree with population totals.\nVery large weights often increase variance; simple trimming changes the\nestimate in turn. One diagnostic is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo>∑<\/mo><msubsup><mi>w<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{eff}}=(\\sum w_i)^2/\\sum w_i^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich reflects weight inequality alone rather than every design effect.\nWeighting does not necessarily resolve an unknown association between\nthe target variable and non-response. Question wording, social\ndesirability, data protection and reporting rules remain important.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> How does the weighted proportion change if B\nprovides only 20 instead of 30 yes answers while everything else remains\nconstant?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>420<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(420+80)/1000=0.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe weights remain unchanged because selection and the total number of\nrespondents are unchanged.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/12-001-x/2024002/article/00013-eng.htm\">Hasler\n(2024): Inference from sampling with response probabilities estimated\nvia calibration<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M30-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M30-L8 – Economics, Forecasts, Information Management and IT Controlling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Data Science in Science and Society<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20, M21, M27, M28.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Align forecasts with decision\ncosts and incorporate information management and IT controlling into\nevaluation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Economic decisions using data<\/h2>\n<p>A retailer wants to derive order quantities from demand forecasts.\nTime series from sales systems do not always represent demand: when\nstock runs out, sales are capped by availability. Prices, promotions and\npublic holidays may be useful features if they are known at forecast\ntime. Data provenance must be documented using item, branch and time\nidentifiers. Information management defines responsibilities, data\nquality, indicators and interfaces; BI dashboards alone do not resolve\ninconsistent definitions. A forecast is economically useful only if it\nleads to an executable decision.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Asymmetric costs and quantile forecasts<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001619_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Expected costs of 120, 76, 48, 36, and 40 euros have a minimum at order quantity 40.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Expected costs of 120, 76, 48, 36, and 40\neuros have a minimum at order quantity 40.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Inventory quantities and expected costs.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In a simplified single-period inventory model, each surplus unit\ncosts €2, while each missing unit costs €6. For order quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand demand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe loss is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(q,d)=2\\max(q-d,0)+6\\max(d-q,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout further constraints, a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=6/(6+2)=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nquantile of the demand distribution minimises expected loss. In the\ncontinuous case, the derivative of expected loss is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2F(q)-6(1-F(q))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich vanishes at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(q)=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor discrete data, the quantile jump must be considered. Five equally\nprobable synthetic demand scenarios are 10, 20, 30, 40 and 50. Ordering\n30 incurs an average cost of €48, ordering 40 incurs €36, and ordering\n50 incurs €40. Of the five candidate quantities, 40 is optimal, not the\nmedian of 30.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">40<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>d[:,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>loss<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.maximum(q<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>d,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.maximum(d<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>q,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)).mean(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(loss,[<span class=\"dv\">120<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">76<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">48<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">36<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">40<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> d[loss.argmin()]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">40<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(loss)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Controlling, backtests and data operations<\/h2>\n<p>Forecast quality should be evaluated on later data not used in\ntraining, including against a simple seasonal reference model. MAE and\nRMSE characterise statistical errors, whereas the loss function above\nevaluates the business decision. IT controlling adds comparable cost\naccounting for development, deployment, data collection, monitoring and\nmaintenance. An expected annual benefit of €20,000 against additional\noperating costs of €12,000 gives a net effect of €8,000 before further\nrisks and taxes. The metric must be documented with clear system\nboundaries, realistic assumptions and scenarios. A historical backtest\ncannot guarantee future profits; real deployment may itself change\nprices and demand.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> How does the target quantile change if surplus\nstock costs €4 while shortage still costs €6?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=6/(4+6)=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe higher surplus penalty lowers the target quantile; the available\norder quantities should be re-evaluated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://otexts.robjhyndman.com/fpp3/toolbox.html\">Hyndman\nand Athanasopoulos: Forecasting: Principles and Practice, 3rd ed.,\nChapter 5<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · section on forecast accuracy opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M30-L9":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L9\">M30-L9 – Industry, Manufacturing and Predictive Maintenance<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Data Science in Science and Society<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20, M21, M27, M28.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish remaining useful\nlife, early warnings and condition-based maintenance, and recognise\ntemporal evaluation boundaries.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From sensor data to maintenance<\/h2>\n<p>Industrial plants monitor temperature, vibration, power consumption\nand throughput. Condition monitoring describes the current state,\nanomaly detection flags unusual patterns, and predictive maintenance\nestimates, for example, the remaining useful life (RUL). High vibration\ncan also result from a change in load; an anomaly does not uniquely\ndiagnose a fault. Equipment identifiers, operating regimes, maintenance\nlogs and timestamps are required. Image-based inspection and process\ndata may be combined for a manufactured part, but a change in product\ntype can shift the data distribution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A simple degradation model<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001549_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Observations up to 40 hours project reaching threshold 9 after another 30 hours, or 20 hours with a steeper trend.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Observations up to 40 hours project\nreaching threshold 9 after another 30 hours, or 20 hours with a steeper\ntrend.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Degradation trends and remaining useful life.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A fictional condition indicator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured at five operating times of 0, 10, 20, 30 and 40 hours, with\nrespective values of 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6. A linear trend\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>z<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat z(t)=2+0.1t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a slope of 0.1 units per hour. If a hypothetical threshold of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis used, extrapolation reaches it at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>70<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=70<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours. With 40 hours already elapsed, the model-based remaining life is\n30 hours. Another plausible increase of 0.15 units per hour from the\ncurrent level of 6 would instead yield a remaining life of only 20\nhours. The threshold must be determined and validated technically; it\ncannot be inferred from a model score alone. Linear extrapolation\nignores accelerated degradation, changes in operating regime and sudden\nfailures.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">41<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> z<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.arange(<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">7.<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b,a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.polyfit(t,z,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> crossing<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rul<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>crossing<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>t[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(b,<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(rul,<span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(a,b,rul,(<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>z[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.15<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Maintenance decisions and independent evaluation<\/h2>\n<p>Condition-based decisions account for failure costs, spare-part\navailability, lead times, occupational safety and measurement\nuncertainty. In a simplified decision example, preventive maintenance\ncosts €200 and an unplanned failure has an expected cost of €1,000. If\nmaintenance completely prevents failure within the relevant window and\nhas no other consequences, it is preferable in expectation when the\nfailure probability exceeds 0.2. Realistic models include imperfect\neffectiveness and further costs. Evaluation separates entire machines\nrather than random time windows, uses only features available at\ndecision time, and documents warning lead time and false-alarm rate.\nMachines that have not failed may provide right-censored observations.\nThe NASA C-MAPSS dataset is a simulation benchmark, not evidence of safe\ndecisions for a real plant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Given a current indicator of 7 and an\nestimated increase of 0.05 units per hour, what remaining time does the\nthreshold of 9 imply?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.05<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(9-7)/0.05=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhours under purely linear extrapolation. This is not a guaranteed safe\nmaintenance date.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nasa.gov/intelligent-systems-division/discovery-and-systems-health/pcoe/pcoe-data-set-repository/\">NASA:\nPrognostics Center of Excellence Data Set Repository<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 6,\nTurbofan Engine Degradation Simulation, opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M30-L10":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L10\">M30-L10 – Chemistry, Materials Science and Data-Driven Material Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Data Science in Science and Society<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20, M21, M27, M28.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Choose representations for\nmaterials data, compute mixture descriptors and test structural\ngeneralisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Molecules, crystals and material properties<\/h2>\n<p>Data-driven chemistry and materials science combine measurements,\nquantum-mechanical simulation and data-based models. A molecule may be\nrepresented as a graph of atoms and bonds; for crystals, periodic\nneighbours and lattices must be considered. Composition vectors are\nsimpler, but contain neither molecular geometry nor crystal structure.\nProperty values are only comparable when measurement conditions match:\ntemperature, pressure, sample preparation, measurement technique and\nunits may all matter. Different simulation levels or laboratories can\nproduce systematically different values. A useful representation must\npreserve the symmetries and physical information relevant to the target\nquantity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Composition descriptor in a teaching example<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001638_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Components at 25% and 75% with descriptor values 2 and 6 yield weighted mean 5.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Components at 25% and 75% with descriptor\nvalues 2 and 6 yield weighted mean 5.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Composition-weighted material descriptor.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For a fictional binary composition with proportions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_A=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_B=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand dimensionless element descriptors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_A=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_B=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe mean descriptor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>d<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar d=\\sum_i c_i d_i=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts weighted dispersion is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>d<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_d=\\sum_i c_i(d_i-\\bar d)^2=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth are invariant to a mere reordering of the list of elements. A model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mover><mi>d<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=10+2\\bar d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields the entirely fictitious property value 20. This formula is\ndeliberately not a material law: a mean descriptor cannot distinguish\nstructures and phases that have different properties. Graph-based models\nfor molecules or crystals may incorporate bonds, distances and local\nenvironments; suitable scalar target quantities should be invariant\nunder translation and rotation.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>c<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.75<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">6.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>c<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>d<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>c<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>((d<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>m)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>m<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(c.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(),<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose([m,v,y],[<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(m,v,y)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Benchmarks and laboratory validation<\/h2>\n<p>Data splits must reflect the research goal. When predicting new\nmolecular families, scaffold-based splits can be more suitable than\nrandom splits; in materials science, composition families, structural\ntypes or chronological discovery sequences can be held out. Duplicate\nentries and near-identical structural variants threaten independence.\nMatbench is one example of a benchmark for material property prediction.\nRobust evaluation includes baseline methods, uncertainty, checks outside\nthe training domain and, where possible, independent experimental\nreplication. A high predicted property establishes neither\nmanufacturability nor stability or safety of a new material.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Compute the mean and weighted dispersion for a\nmixture containing equal proportions of the two element descriptors.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>d<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar d=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_d=0.5(2-4)^2+0.5(6-4)^2=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe changed dispersion shows that the mean alone does not summarise\nevery feature.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.00707\">Dunn et al. (2020):\nBenchmarking Materials Property Prediction Methods<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M30-L11":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L11\">M30-L11 – AI for Scientific Discovery and Hypothesis-Driven Experiments<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Data Science in Science and Society<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20, M21, M27, M28.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Plan adaptive experiments using\nan acquisition function and distinguish computational hypothesis\ngeneration from confirmation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>AI as a tool for scientific discovery<\/h2>\n<p>Scientific experiments are often expensive: a laboratory can measure\nonly a few samples per day, while a simulation may require many\ncomputing hours. Rather than investigate every candidate, a surrogate\nmodel is trained on existing measurements. Active learning chooses\nfurther data to improve a knowledge or modelling task, while Bayesian\noptimisation directs the selection towards an optimisation objective\n(M18, M23). These approaches can overlap. A model may also suggest\nhypotheses about mechanisms or relationships. Such suggestions do not\nautomatically constitute discoveries: a hypothesis formulated in advance\nneeds measurable and falsifiable consequences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exploration and exploitation in a numerical example<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001569_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Mean rewards 7, 8, and 6 produce UCB scores 8, 8.4, and 10, selecting candidate C.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Mean rewards 7, 8, and 6 produce UCB\nscores 8, 8.4, and 10, selecting candidate C.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Upper confidence bound for experiment selection.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Three harmless, fictional simulation parameters A, B and C are\ncandidates. A surrogate predicts dimensionless target means\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=(7,8,6)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand standard deviations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=(0.5,0.2,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe maximise an illustrative upper confidence bound (UCB) score\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a(x)=\\mu(x)+\\kappa\\sigma(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa\\geq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nB would be the first choice. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe scores are 8, 8.4 and 10; C is chosen because of its uncertainty.\nThe surrogate must be updated after the measurement. For a normal\ndistribution, an upper quantile limit can be represented using a\nsuitable quantile multiplier; an arbitrarily chosen UCB, however, is not\na guaranteed confidence bound. The choice of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>κ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermines the relative importance of exploration here and does not\nguarantee finding the global optimum.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>mu<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">7.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">8.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">6.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> sigma<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>score<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>mu<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sigma<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(score,[<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">8.4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> score.argmax()<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> mu.argmax()<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(score)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From adaptive screening to confirmation<\/h2>\n<p>Before searching, specify admissible candidates, budget, measurement\nprocess, quality rules and target metric. After every measurement,\nrecord unsuccessful attempts and deviations too. Safety and feasibility\nlimits must not be overridden by a high acquisition score. With multiple\nobjectives, a Pareto set may be more useful than a single maximum.\nRepeatedly selecting the best candidate from noisy data can introduce\nwinner’s bias. After exploratory selection, an independent confirmatory\nexperiment with a prespecified analysis is therefore necessary; adaptive\ninterim analyses require suitable statistical procedures. AI may reduce\nexperiment costs, but scientific explanations require additional tests\nof competing hypotheses, uncertainty and reproducibility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Calculate the UCB scores when κ=0.5. Which\ncandidate is now selected?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> A: 7.25; B: 8.1; C: 7. B is selected. The\nchange follows from giving uncertainty less weight.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11831-024-10064-z\">Di\nFiore, Nardelli and Mainini (2024): Active Learning and Bayesian\nOptimization<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · introduction and classification opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
